B[2] = ("1.2", "Forensic Auditing & the Therac-25", [
 Q("Forensic audit","A forensic audit answers which two separate questions?","What technically went wrong, and who is responsible for it",["How much the device cost, and who bought it","How fast the device was, and who made it","When the device was sold, and where it was used"],"Those are not the same question."),
 Q("Forensic audit","Are the technical cause and who is responsible the same question?","No. They can point in different directions",["Yes. The cause always names the person responsible","Yes. A forensic audit only asks one question","No. Only the technical question matters"],"Technical root cause and accountability are separate."),
 Q("Forensic audit","What does a forensic audit rely on?","Evidence, not assumptions or guesses",["Opinions about who looks the guiltiest","The first story that the news reports","A vote among the people at the company"],"It follows the facts where they lead."),
 Q("Therac-25","What was the Therac-25?","A computer-controlled radiation therapy machine from the mid-1980s",["A heart monitor that was built for home use","A robot arm that was built to assemble cars","A scanner that was built for airport security"],"It delivered treatment two ways."),
 Q("Therac-25","When did the Therac-25 overdoses happen?","Between 1985 and 1987",["Between 1965 and 1967","Between 1995 and 1997","Between 2005 and 2007"],"At least six incidents are documented."),
 Q("Therac-25","At least how many documented overdose incidents were there?","Six",["One","Two","Fifty"],"They caused severe burns and multiple deaths."),
 Q("Therac-25","About how large were the overdoses?","Up to roughly 100 times the intended dose",["About twice the intended dose","About half the intended dose","Exactly the intended dose each time"],"The doses were massive."),
 Q("Therac-25","What were the Therac-25's two treatment modes?","A low-power electron beam, and a high-power X-ray beam that needs a beam spreader",["A heat mode and a cold mode for the patient","A fast mode and a slow mode for the operator","A sound mode and a light mode for the room"],"The X-ray mode needed a physical attachment in place first."),
 Q("Therac-25","Why did the high-power beam need a spreader in place?","The concentrated beam was only safe once it was spread out",["The spreader made the machine run faster","The spreader kept the machine from overheating","The spreader was needed to show the dose"],"Without it the beam hit the patient directly."),
 Q("Safety-critical","What is a safety-critical system?","One where a failure can directly cause serious injury or death",["One that is only used by the police","One that has the most expensive parts","One that is used only inside hospitals"],"The Therac-25 is the defining example."),
 Q("Real-time","What is a real-time system?","One where the timing of an action matters as much as the action itself",["One that shows the current time on a screen","One that is always connected to the internet","One that runs only while a person watches it"],"A correct response that is too late is a failure."),
 Q("Race condition","What is a race condition?","A bug where the result depends on the timing or order of two things",["A bug caused by a broken wire inside a machine","A bug that makes a program run too slowly","A bug caused by a user typing a wrong password"],"The timing is not properly controlled."),
 Q("Race condition","What two things raced in the Therac-25 bug?","The operator's quick typing, and the software's safety check",["Two operators using the same machine","Two beams firing at the same time","The power supply and the cooling fan"],"Typing fast enough won the race."),
 Q("Race condition","Within about how many seconds did an operator have to type a correction for the bug?","About 8 seconds",["About 8 minutes","About 8 hours","About 8 milliseconds"],"A fast, experienced operator could do it."),
 Q("Interlock","What is a hardware interlock?","A physical safeguard that makes a dangerous state impossible, whatever the software does",["A software check that asks the user to confirm","A password that locks the operator's screen","A cable that joins the machine to a computer"],"It does not depend on code."),
 Q("Interlock","Which earlier machines had a hardware interlock?","The Therac-6 and the Therac-20",["The Therac-25 and the Therac-30","Only the machines made after 1990","None of the earlier machines had one"],"The Therac-25 removed it."),
 Q("Redundancy","What is redundancy?","More than one independent safety check for the same danger",["A single very strong safety check for a danger","Repeating the same bug in many parts of a program","Using the cheapest parts so a machine costs less"],"One failing does not take the whole system down."),
 Q("SPOF","What is a single point of failure?","One part that, if it fails, takes down everything that depends on it",["A part that is placed at the very start of a device","A part that was made by only one company","A part that is used only one time in a program"],"There is nothing else to catch the failure."),
 Q("Fail-safe","What is a fail-safe design?","If something goes wrong, the system defaults to a safe state",["If something goes wrong, the system keeps going faster","If something goes wrong, the operator must fix it by hand","If something goes wrong, the system hides the message"],"The Therac-25 was not fail-safe."),
 Q("Firmware","What role did firmware play in the Therac-25 overdoses?","The race condition lived in the firmware that checked the beam spreader",["The firmware was copied from a video game","The firmware was never installed in the machine","The firmware only controlled the room lights"],"It decided in real time whether the spreader was in place."),
 # ---- practice test
 Q("Why it matters","Why does the Therac-25 anchor the whole course?","Software alone is often not enough protection for a safety-critical system",["Because it was the first computer ever built","Because it was made by the largest company","Because it was the cheapest machine on the market"],"A second, physical layer catches what software misses."),
 Q("Interlock","What did the Therac-25 remove from the older designs?","The hardware interlock",["The beam spreader","The electron beam","The operator's keyboard"],"It relied on software alone."),
 Q("SPOF","Once the interlock was gone, what did the software safety check become?","A single point of failure",["A redundant backup","A fail-safe","A hardware interlock"],"Nothing else was left to catch its failure."),
 Q("Bug","When did the Therac-25 bug appear?","When an operator typed unusually fast, something testers had not tried",["Only when the machine was turned off","Only when two patients used it at once","Every single time the machine was switched on"],"That timing is why it is a race condition."),
 Q("Auditing","What should a forensic auditor do with an unpopular conclusion?","Follow the evidence, even if it points at the company paying for the audit",["Change the conclusion to keep the company happy","Leave out the facts that nobody likes","Wait until the news has stopped covering it"],"The goal is evidence, not comfort."),
 Q("Parties","Why might a real investigation look at the engineer, the company and the user?","A failure can have more than one contributing factor and party",["Because the user is always the one who is at fault","Because the engineer is never responsible","Because the law only allows three names in a report"],"Real failures are chains."),
 Q("Fail-safe","Which sentence describes a fail-safe design?","If something goes wrong, it defaults to a safe state",["It never has any bugs inside it","It checks itself only once a year","It stops working when it is touched"],"Failing safely is a design choice."),
 Q("Real-time","Which sentence describes a real-time system?","Timing matters as much as the action itself",["It always uses the newest chip available","It has to be connected to a clock on the wall","It shows the time of day to the operator"],"A late right answer can be a failure."),
])
